1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,893
A speedometer reads forty kilometres per
hour, right now, at this one instant.

2
00:00:05,293 --> 00:00:07,854
But speed is distance divided by time.

3
00:00:07,854 --> 00:00:10,574
In one instant, no time passes at all.

4
00:00:11,024 --> 00:00:14,163
Diya noticed this on the speed
meter of her bicycle this morning.

5
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:16,524
Zero distance over zero time.

6
00:00:16,524 --> 00:00:19,067
So how can the meter show any speed?

7
00:00:19,567 --> 00:00:22,392
The answer is one of the
biggest ideas in maths.

8
00:00:22,392 --> 00:00:26,547
It is called the derivative, and today
we build it from first principles.

9
00:00:27,197 --> 00:00:31,139
Let us test it on a falling ball,
the example from our NCERT book.

10
00:00:31,539 --> 00:00:35,841
A ball dropped from a cliff falls
four point nine t squared metres in t

11
00:00:35,841 --> 00:00:36,741
seconds.

12
00:00:36,919 --> 00:00:39,927
How fast is it going
at exactly two seconds?

13
00:00:39,927 --> 00:00:42,049
We cannot divide by zero time.

14
00:00:42,049 --> 00:00:44,282
So we take a small gap instead.

15
00:00:44,682 --> 00:00:49,121
From two seconds to three seconds,
the average speed is twenty four point

16
00:00:49,121 --> 00:00:50,711
five metres per second.

17
00:00:51,111 --> 00:00:52,451
Now shrink the gap.

18
00:00:52,451 --> 00:00:55,521
A tenth of a second gives
twenty point zero nine.

19
00:00:55,521 --> 00:00:58,387
A hundredth gives nineteen
point six four nine.

20
00:00:58,787 --> 00:01:00,400
Watch the number settle.

21
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:05,018
As the gap shrinks towards zero, the
speed gets closer and closer to nineteen

22
00:01:05,018 --> 00:01:05,918
point six.

23
00:01:05,848 --> 00:01:08,062
That is the speed at one instant.

24
00:01:08,712 --> 00:01:10,827
Now let us do this for any curve.

25
00:01:10,827 --> 00:01:14,454
Think of the curve as a hill,
and you are walking along it.

26
00:01:14,854 --> 00:01:15,754
Step one.

27
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:16,969
Stand at a point x.

28
00:01:16,969 --> 00:01:18,855
Your height there is f of x.

29
00:01:19,605 --> 00:01:20,505
Step two.

30
00:01:20,441 --> 00:01:23,175
Take a small step forward, of size h.

31
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:25,663
Your new height is f of x plus h.

32
00:01:26,413 --> 00:01:27,485
Step three.

33
00:01:27,485 --> 00:01:31,064
The rise is f of x plus h, minus f of x.

34
00:01:31,064 --> 00:01:32,755
The run is just h.

35
00:01:33,505 --> 00:01:34,405
Step four.

36
00:01:34,343 --> 00:01:38,514
Rise over run is the steepness of
the line joining the two points.

37
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:40,499
This line is called a secant.

38
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:42,149
Step five.

39
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:43,859
Now let h shrink to zero.

40
00:01:43,859 --> 00:01:48,285
The secant turns into the tangent,
a line that just touches the curve.

41
00:01:49,035 --> 00:01:53,481
The limit of rise over run is the
derivative, written f dash of x.

42
00:01:53,481 --> 00:01:56,739
Finding it this way is
called the first principle.

43
00:01:57,389 --> 00:01:58,439
Side by side.

44
00:01:58,439 --> 00:02:00,998
Average speed uses a real gap of time.

45
00:02:00,998 --> 00:02:03,965
On the graph, it is the slope of a secant.

46
00:02:04,365 --> 00:02:07,426
Speed at one instant lets
the gap shrink to zero.

47
00:02:07,426 --> 00:02:10,394
On the graph, it is the
slope of the tangent.

48
00:02:10,394 --> 00:02:11,915
That is the derivative.

49
00:02:12,515 --> 00:02:13,415
Your turn.

50
00:02:13,329 --> 00:02:17,192
In the fraction, can we just
put h equal to zero right away?

51
00:02:17,192 --> 00:02:18,343
Pause and think.

52
00:02:21,543 --> 00:02:22,443
No!

53
00:02:21,978 --> 00:02:25,046
The top becomes zero and
the bottom becomes zero.

54
00:02:25,046 --> 00:02:27,138
Zero over zero tells us nothing.

55
00:02:27,138 --> 00:02:29,921
We simplify first, then let h go to zero.

56
00:02:30,571 --> 00:02:31,587
Remember this.

57
00:02:31,587 --> 00:02:36,242
Take a tiny step, divide by the step,
then let the step shrink to zero.

58
00:02:36,942 --> 00:02:38,632
Now, one worked example.

59
00:02:38,982 --> 00:02:41,665
Find the derivative of x
squared from first principles.

60
00:02:42,165 --> 00:02:43,065
Step one.

61
00:02:43,039 --> 00:02:46,194
f of x plus h is x plus h, all squared.

62
00:02:46,194 --> 00:02:49,936
That is x squared, plus
two x h, plus h squared.

63
00:02:50,686 --> 00:02:51,586
Step two.

64
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:52,909
Subtract f of x.

65
00:02:52,909 --> 00:02:57,913
The x squared cancels, and we are
left with two x h, plus h squared.

66
00:02:58,663 --> 00:02:59,598
Step three.

67
00:02:59,598 --> 00:03:00,644
Divide by h.

68
00:03:00,644 --> 00:03:02,432
We get two x, plus h.

69
00:03:03,182 --> 00:03:04,082
Step four.

70
00:03:04,038 --> 00:03:05,671
Now let h go to zero.

71
00:03:05,671 --> 00:03:08,210
The derivative of x squared is two x.

72
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,537
Check it on the curve.

73
00:03:10,537 --> 00:03:15,363
At x equal to three, the tangent climbs
six units for every one unit across.

74
00:03:16,013 --> 00:03:17,478
Now, the exam trap.

75
00:03:17,828 --> 00:03:23,277
For f of x equal to x squared, many
students write f of x plus h as x squared

76
00:03:23,277 --> 00:03:24,177
plus h.

77
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:28,770
But f of x plus h means we
put x plus h in place of x.

78
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:31,406
So it is x plus h, all squared.

79
00:03:31,406 --> 00:03:36,611
With x equal to three and h equal
to one, that is sixteen, not ten.

80
00:03:37,261 --> 00:03:38,797
Where is this asked?

81
00:03:38,797 --> 00:03:39,859
In JEE Main.

82
00:03:39,859 --> 00:03:44,593
In March twenty twenty one, a paper
hid a derivative inside a limit, f

83
00:03:44,593 --> 00:03:48,212
of h minus one, over h,
and asked for its value.

84
00:03:48,812 --> 00:03:50,036
Exam level now.

85
00:03:50,036 --> 00:03:53,417
For f of x equal to x
cubed, find this limit.

86
00:03:53,417 --> 00:03:54,692
Pause and solve.

87
00:04:00,092 --> 00:04:01,485
The answer is C.

88
00:04:01,485 --> 00:04:02,533
Twenty four.

89
00:04:03,033 --> 00:04:04,558
Expand both cubes.

90
00:04:04,558 --> 00:04:10,291
Two plus h, cubed, minus two minus
h, cubed, is twenty four h, plus two

91
00:04:10,291 --> 00:04:11,191
h cubed.

92
00:04:11,673 --> 00:04:12,726
Divide by h.

93
00:04:12,726 --> 00:04:15,568
We get twenty four, plus two h squared.

94
00:04:16,068 --> 00:04:19,760
Let h go to zero, and the
answer is twenty four.

95
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:24,859
That is twice f dash of two, because
the gap here is two h, not h.

96
00:04:25,459 --> 00:04:27,000
Here is your recap.

97
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,584
Screenshot this card.

98
00:04:31,784 --> 00:04:33,970
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

99
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:35,270
One.

100
00:04:34,835 --> 00:04:37,900
Why can we not put h equal
to zero straight away?

101
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:39,700
Two.

102
00:04:39,177 --> 00:04:41,717
Find the derivative of three
x from first principles.

103
00:04:42,617 --> 00:04:43,517
Three.

104
00:04:43,087 --> 00:04:45,353
What does the slope of
the tangent tell us?

105
00:04:46,453 --> 00:04:48,962
Next time: the derivative
of a sum is easy.

106
00:04:48,962 --> 00:04:52,741
But is the derivative of a product
just the product of the derivatives?

107
00:04:52,741 --> 00:04:55,077
We find out in the algebra of derivatives.
